The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (sch->driver_override && strcmp(sch->driver_override, drv->name))
```
```c
ret = driver_set_override(dev, &sch->driver_override, buf, count);
```
Reason: In `css_bus_match()`, the driver_override pointer is read without holding the device lock, while `driver_override_store()` (via `driver_set_override()`) modifies the same pointer under the device lock. A concurrent write can free or change the string, causing a use-after-free when the unguarded `strcmp` accesses the dangling pointer.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
ret = device_match_driver_override(dev, drv);
if (ret == 0)
	return 0;
```
```c
static const struct bus_type css_bus_type = {
	...
	.driver_override = true,
	...
};
```
(Corresponding removal of manual `kfree(sch->driver_override)` and custom sysfs attributes are also part of the fix.)
Reason: `device_match_driver_override()` internally acquires the device lock to safely compare the driver override string, preventing the race condition. Setting `bus->driver_override = true` tells the driver core to manage the `driver_override` field automatically (including cleanup in `device_release`), replacing the manual memory management and custom sysfs store/show operations.

